  // Mental models and thinking tools
  "The map is never the territory, a model of reality is always a simplification, useful mostly for what it deliberately leaves out.",
  "First principles thinking means breaking a hard problem down to its most basic true parts instead of just tweaking whatever's already assumed.",
  "Inversion flips a hard question around, instead of asking how to succeed, it asks what would guarantee failure, and works backward from there.",
  "Occam's razor favors the simplest explanation that fits the facts, right up until the facts themselves demand something more complicated.",
  "Hanlon's razor suggests assuming incompetence before malice, most bad outcomes turn out to be mistakes, not conspiracies.",
  "Second order thinking asks not just what happens next, but what happens after that, before committing to a decision.",
  "A circle of competence marks the honest edge of what you actually understand well, and the discipline is mostly just staying inside it.",
  "Regression to the mean quietly explains why an unusually good or bad result is rarely repeated, extremes tend to drift back toward average.",
  "A feedback loop is any system where the output quietly becomes the next input, which is how small changes turn into either growth or collapse.",
  "The law of diminishing returns explains why the first hour of effort on almost anything matters more than the tenth.",
  "A bottleneck is the one narrow point in a system where a small fix creates an outsized improvement everywhere downstream.",
  "Margin of safety is the deliberate buffer built into a plan specifically for the moment reality doesn't go the way anyone expected.",
  "Critical mass is the exact point where slow, unnoticed change suddenly turns into fast, obvious change.",
  "Emergence describes how a system can behave in ways none of its individual pieces would predict on their own.",
  "Multiplying by zero is a blunt but useful reminder that one critical failure can erase the value of everything else you did right.",
  "A thought experiment lets you test an idea entirely in your head, without the cost or risk of actually trying it in the real world.",
  "Probabilistic thinking treats most decisions as bets, not certainties, and focuses on the odds instead of demanding a guaranteed outcome.",
  "Scarcity doesn't just describe limited resources, it actively shapes value and urgency, how badly people want the thing that's running out.",
  "A trade-off is simply the honest admission that every choice quietly closes off some other choice at the same time.",
  "Specialization trades breadth for depth, betting that going narrow and deep beats spreading the same effort thin across everything.",
  "Interdependence means strength and vulnerability tend to arrive together, since relying on others is also a kind of exposure.",
  "Creative destruction is the uncomfortable idea that real progress usually requires something older and functional to be replaced entirely.",
  "A system under too much efficiency often becomes brittle, since removing every bit of slack also removes its ability to absorb a shock.",
  "An incentive is often a far better predictor of behavior than a stated value, people quietly follow whichever one actually gets rewarded.",
  "Ecosystem thinking assumes nothing exists in true isolation, every part is quietly shaped by, and shaping, everything around it.",
  "A niche is simply the specific space where competition is lowest, which is often more valuable than being the best in a crowded one.",
  "One classic effect describes having to keep improving constantly just to maintain the exact same relative position as everyone else.",
  "Leverage is the idea that a small, well placed force can move something far larger than its size would ever suggest.",
  "Activation energy is the extra push needed just to get something moving, after that the effort required usually drops considerably.",
  "A catalyst speeds up a process dramatically without permanently changing itself in the process.",
  "Global versus local maxima is the uncomfortable idea that the best nearby option might still be far worse than one you haven't found yet.",
  "Equilibrium describes a balance of opposing forces, though real systems rarely sit there for long before something shifts it again.",
  "Randomness is something people are famously bad at recognizing, we tend to see patterns in noise far more often than noise in patterns.",
  "A monopoly and real competition pull a market in opposite directions, and most industries sit somewhere uneasily between the two.",
  "One old economic law observes that inferior options can quietly dominate a market once nothing punishes choosing them over better ones.",
  "Framing doesn't change the facts of a situation, it changes which facts a person notices first, which quietly changes everything after.",
  "Contrast is often what makes people actually notice something, a detail rarely stands out until it's placed next to its opposite.",
  "An algorithm, at its simplest, is just a repeatable recipe, a fixed sequence of steps reliably producing the same kind of result.",
  "Reciprocity is the quiet rule underneath most relationships, people tend to mirror whatever they're given, generosity included.",
  "Churn describes healthy turnover in a system, useful in moderation and quietly dangerous the moment it accelerates too far.",

